g but can be updated via decisions; the baton is just a session instruction and not binding beyond that.\n\nLet's search for \"precedence\" in the docs. In `docs/current-mental-model/Bets.md` there's \"Authority rules\". Not about precedence.\n\nIn `docs/current-mental-model/decs`? Not.\n\nIn `docs/current-mental-model/_map.md` maybe.\n\nSearch for \"precedence\" in the provided text: I see \"precedence\" in the retro section: \"the precedence rule (baton item 9)\". Also \"precedence: decisions.md wins; STANDING line is superseded\". In the BATON file: \"precedence: decisions.md wins; STANDING's 'one phase per FRESH session' line is superseded\". So there is mention that decisions.md wins over standing rules. That suggests decision log has higher precedence than the standing rules (like the STANDING file). But the question is about contract vs baton vs decision.\n\nIn the BATON doc: \"precedence: decisions.md wins; STANDING’s 'one phase per FRESH session’ line is superseded\". So decision doc outranks the standing rule. That suggests that decision log is higher precedence than other docs like STANDING. But what about contract? It might be that the contract is also a binding doc but is subordinate to decision log? Or maybe the contract is also binding but can be changed only via decisions.\n\nLet's examine the \"Authority note\" sections in the contracts: They say \"Ground truth used here: decisions.md, ...\". That suggests that the decision log is the source of truth for the contract. So the precedence order might be: decision log > contract > batan? Or maybe contract and decision are both binding, but the contract is derived from decisions, so decisions outrank contract. The baton is a session instruction that can override everything else (it says supersedes everything below). So the hierarchy: baton (highest), then decision (next), then contract (lowest)? Or maybe the order is: baton > contract > decision? Let's think.\n\nThe baton is basically a \"READ FIRST; sup